  "id": "Compound::DB00761 - Gene::6557",
  "verbalization": "Potassium Chloride is shown to interact with multiple members of the Slc12 family, specifically Slc12a5, Slc12a6, and Slc12a7, which are all involved in cation-coupled chloride cotransporters. These transporters are critical for the movement of chloride ions across cellular membranes in response to cations, a process essential for maintaining cellular homeostasis. The evidence suggests that Potassium Chloride may influence the function of these transporters, potentially modulating chloride transport across cell membranes. The high scores from the three paths indicate a strong and consistent interaction between Potassium Chloride and Slc12a1, which is part of the same pathway as the other Slc12 members. This suggests that Potassium Chloride may play a role in regulating the activity of these transporters, possibly through direct binding or by influencing their expression or function. The evidence supports a mechanistic link between Potassium Chloride and Slc12a1, highlighting the importance of these transporters in cellular chloride homeostasis.",
